19	## Table of Contents
20	- [Features](#features)
21	- [How to install](#how-to-install)
22	- [Getting Started](#getting-started)
23	- [Usage](#usage)
24	- [Handling Long-Running Commands](#handling-long-running-commands)
25	- [Work in Progress and TODOs](#roadmap)
26	- [Sponsors and Supporters](#support-desktop-commander)
27	- [Website](#website)
28	- [Media](#media)
29	- [Testimonials](#testimonials)
30	- [Frequently Asked Questions](#frequently-asked-questions)
31	- [Contributing](#contributing)
32	- [License](#license)
33	
34	All of your AI development tools in one place.
35	Desktop Commander puts all dev tools in one chat.
36	Execute long-running terminal commands on your computer and manage processes through Model Context Protocol (MCP). Built on top of [MCP Filesystem Server](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers/tree/main/src/filesystem) to provide additional search and replace file editing capabilities.
37	
38	## Features
39	
40	- **Enhanced terminal commands with interactive process control**
41	- **Execute code in memory (Python, Node.js, R) without saving files**
42	- **Instant data analysis - just ask to analyze CSV/JSON files**
43	- **Interact with running processes (SSH, databases, development servers)**
44	- Execute terminal commands with output streaming
45	- Command timeout and background execution support
46	- Process management (list and kill processes)
47	- Session management for long-running commands
48	- Server configuration management:
49	  - Get/set configuration values
50	  - Update multiple settings at once
51	  - Dynamic configuration changes without server restart
52	- Full filesystem operations:
53	  - Read/write files
54	  - Create/list directories
55	  - Move files/directories
56	  - Search files
57	  - Get file metadata
58	  - **Negative offset file reading**: Read from end of files using negative offset values (like Unix tail)
59	- Code editing capabilities:
60	  - Surgical text replacements for small changes
61	  - Full file rewrites for major changes
62	  - Multiple file support
63	  - Pattern-based replacements
64	  - vscode-ripgrep based recursive code or text search in folders
65	- Comprehensive audit logging:
66	  - All tool calls are automatically logged
67	  - Log rotation with 10MB size limit
68	  - Detailed timestamps and arguments
69	
70	## How to install
71	
72	Desktop Commander offers multiple installation methods to fit different user needs and technical requirements.
73	
74	> **📋 Update & Uninstall Information:** Before choosing an installation option, note that **only Options 1, 2, 3, and 6 have automatic updates**. Options 4 and 5 require manual updates. See the sections below for update and uninstall instructions for each option.
75	
76	### Option 1: Install through npx ⭐ **Auto-Updates** **Requires Node.js**
77	Just run this in terminal:
78	```
79	npx @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest setup
80	```
81	
82	For debugging mode (allows Node.js inspector connection):
83	```
84	npx @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest setup --debug
85	```
86	
87	**Command line options during setup:**
88	- `--debug`: Enable debugging mode for Node.js inspector
89	- `--no-onboarding`: Disable onboarding prompts for new users
90	
91	Restart Claude if running.
92	
93	**✅ Auto-Updates:** Yes - automatically updates when you restart Claude  
94	**🔄 Manual Update:** Run the setup command again  
95	**🗑️ Uninstall:** Run `npx @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest remove`
96	
97	### Option 2: Using bash script installer (macOS) ⭐ **Auto-Updates** **Installs Node.js if needed**
98	For macOS users, you can use our automated bash installer which will check your Node.js version, install it if needed, and automatically configure Desktop Commander:
99	```
100	curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install.sh | bash
101	```
102	This script handles all dependencies and configuration automatically for a seamless setup experience.
103	
104	**✅ Auto-Updates:** Yes - requires manual updates  
105	**🔄 Manual Update:** Re-run the bash installer command above  
106	**🗑️ Uninstall:** Run `npx @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest remove`
107	
108	### Option 3: Installing via Smithery ⭐ **Auto-Updates** **Requires Node.js**
109	
110	To install Desktop Commander for Claude Desktop via [Smithery](https://smithery.ai/server/@wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander):
111	
112	1. **Visit the Smithery page:** https://smithery.ai/server/@wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander
113	2. **Login to Smithery** if you haven't already
114	3. **Select your client** (Claude Desktop) on the right side
115	4. **Install with the provided key** that appears after selecting your client
116	5. **Restart Claude Desktop**
117	
118	The old command-line installation method is no longer supported. Please use the web interface above for the most reliable installation experience.
119	
120	**✅ Auto-Updates:** Yes - automatically updates when you restart Claude  
121	**🔄 Manual Update:** Visit the Smithery page and reinstall  
122	
123	### Option 4: Add to claude_desktop_config manually ⭐ **Auto-Updates** **Requires Node.js**
124	Add this entry to your claude_desktop_config.json:
125	
126	- On Mac: `~/Library/Application\ Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`
127	- On Windows: `%APPDATA%\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json`
128	- On Linux: `~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`
129	
130	```json
131	{
132	  "mcpServers": {
133	    "desktop-commander": {
134	      "command": "npx",
135	      "args": [
136	        "-y",
137	        "@wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest"
138	      ]
139	    }
140	  }
141	}
142	```
143	Restart Claude if running.
144	
145	**✅ Auto-Updates:** Yes - automatically updates when you restart Claude  
146	**🔄 Manual Update:** Run the setup command again  
147	**🗑️ Uninstall:** Run `npx @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest remove` or  remove the "desktop-commander" entry from your claude_desktop_config.json file
148	
149	### ### Option 5: Checkout locally ❌ **Manual Updates** **Requires Node.js** ❌ **Manual Updates** **Requires Node.js**
150	1. Clone and build:
151	```bash
152	git clone https://github.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP.git
153	cd DesktopCommanderMCP
154	npm run setup
155	```
156	Restart Claude if running.
157	
158	The setup command will:
159	- Install dependencies
160	- Build the server
161	- Configure Claude's desktop app
162	- Add MCP servers to Claude's config if needed
163	
164	**❌ Auto-Updates:** No - requires manual git updates  
165	**🔄 Manual Update:** `cd DesktopCommanderMCP && git pull && npm run setup`  
166	**🗑️ Uninstall:** Run `npx @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest remove` or remove the cloned directory and remove MCP server entry from Claude config
167	
168	### Option 6: Docker Installation 🐳 ⭐ **Auto-Updates** **No Node.js Required**
169	
170	Perfect for users who want complete or partial isolation or don't have Node.js installed. Desktop Commander runs in a sandboxed Docker container with a persistent work environment.
171	
172	#### Prerequisites
173	- [Docker Desktop](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/) installed **and running**
174	- Claude Desktop app installed
175	
176	**Important:** Make sure Docker Desktop is fully started before running the installer.
177	
178	#### Automated Installation (Recommended)
179	
180	**macOS/Linux:**
181	```bash
182	bash <(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install-docker.sh)
183	```
184	
185	**Windows PowerShell:**
186	```powershell
187	# Download and run the installer (one-liner)
188	iex ((New-Object System.Net.WebClient).DownloadString('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install-docker.ps1'))
189	```
190	
191	The automated installer will:
192	- Check Docker installation
193	- Pull the latest Docker image 
194	- Prompt you to select folders for mounting
195	- Configure Claude Desktop automatically
196	- Restart Claude if possible
197	
198	#### How Docker Persistence Works
199	Desktop Commander creates a persistent work environment that remembers everything between sessions:
200	- **Your development tools**: Any software you install (Node.js, Python, databases, etc.) stays installed
201	- **Your configurations**: Git settings, SSH keys, shell preferences, and other personal configs are preserved  
202	- **Your work files**: Projects and files in the workspace area persist across restarts
203	- **Package caches**: Downloaded packages and dependencies are cached for faster future installs
204	
205	Think of it like having your own dedicated development computer that never loses your setup, but runs safely isolated from your main system.
206	
207	#### Manual Docker Configuration
208	
209	If you prefer manual setup, add this to your claude_desktop_config.json:
210	
211	**Basic setup (no file access):**
212	```json
213	{
214	  "mcpServers": {
215	    "desktop-commander-in-docker": {
216	      "command": "docker",
217	      "args": [
218	        "run",
219	        "-i",
220	        "--rm",
221	        "mcp/desktop-commander:latest"
222	      ]
223	    }
224	  }
225	}
226	```
227	
228	**With folder mounting:**
229	```json
230	{
231	  "mcpServers": {
232	    "desktop-commander-in-docker": {
233	      "command": "docker",
234	      "args": [
235	        "run",
236	        "-i",
237	        "--rm",
238	        "-v", "/Users/username/Desktop:/mnt/desktop",
239	        "-v", "/Users/username/Documents:/mnt/documents",
240	        "mcp/desktop-commander:latest"
241	      ]
242	    }
243	  }
244	}
245	```
246	
247	**Advanced folder mounting:**
248	```json
249	{
250	  "mcpServers": {
251	    "desktop-commander-in-docker": {
252	      "command": "docker",
253	      "args": [
254	        "run", "-i", "--rm",
255	        "-v", "dc-system:/usr",
256	        "-v", "dc-home:/root", 
257	        "-v", "dc-workspace:/workspace",
258	        "-v", "dc-packages:/var",
259	        "-v", "/Users/username/Projects:/mnt/Projects",
260	        "-v", "/Users/username/Downloads:/mnt/Downloads",
261	        "mcp/desktop-commander:latest"
262	      ]
263	    }
264	  }
265	}
266	```
267	
268	#### Docker Benefits
269	✅ **Controlled Isolation:** Runs in sandboxed environment with persistent development state
270	✅ **No Node.js Required:** Everything included in the container
271	✅ **Cross-Platform:** Same experience on all operating systems
272	✅ **Persistent Environment:** Your tools, files, configs, and work survives restarts
273	
274	**✅ Auto-Updates:** Yes - `latest` tag automatically gets newer versions  
275	**🔄 Manual Update:** `docker pull mcp/desktop-commander:latest` then restart Claude  
276	
277	#### Docker Management Commands
278	
279	**macOS/Linux:**
280	
281	Check installation status:
282	```bash
283	bash <(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install-docker.sh) --status
284	```
285	
286	Reset all persistent data (removes all installed tools and configs):
287	```bash
288	bash <(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install-docker.sh) --reset
289	```
290	
291	**Windows PowerShell:**
292	
293	Check status:
294	```powershell
295	$script = (New-Object System.Net.WebClient).DownloadString('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install-docker.ps1'); & ([ScriptBlock]::Create("$script")) -Status
296	```
297	
298	Reset all data:
299	```powershell
300	$script = (New-Object System.Net.WebClient).DownloadString('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install-docker.ps1'); & ([ScriptBlock]::Create("$script")) -Reset
301	```
302	
303	Show help:
304	```powershell
305	$script = (New-Object System.Net.WebClient).DownloadString('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install-docker.ps1'); & ([ScriptBlock]::Create("$script")) -Help
306	```
307	
308	Verbose output:
309	```powershell
310	$script = (New-Object System.Net.WebClient).DownloadString('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install-docker.ps1'); & ([ScriptBlock]::Create("$script")) -VerboseOutput
311	```  
312	
313	#### Troubleshooting Docker Installation
314	If you broke the Docker container or need a fresh start:
315	```bash
316	# Reset and reinstall from scratch
317	bash <(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install-docker.sh) --reset && bash <(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/wonderwhy-er/DesktopCommanderMCP/refs/heads/main/install-docker.sh)
318	```
319	This will completely reset your persistent environment and reinstall everything fresh with exception of not touching mounted folders
320	
321	## Updating & Uninstalling Desktop Commander
322	
323	### Automatic Updates (Options 1, 2, 3, 4 & 6)
324	**Options 1 (npx), Option 2 (bash installer), 3 (Smithery), 4 (manual config), and 6 (Docker)** automatically update to the latest version whenever you restart Claude. No manual intervention needed.
325	
326	### Manual Updates (Option 5)
327	- **Option 5 (local checkout):** `cd DesktopCommanderMCP && git pull && npm run setup`
328	
329	### Uninstalling Desktop Commander
330	#### 🤖 Automatic Uninstallation (Recommended)
331	
332	The easiest way to completely remove Desktop Commander:
333	
334	```bash
335	npx @wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest remove
336	```
337	
338	This automatic uninstaller will:
339	- ✅ Remove Desktop Commander from Claude's MCP server configuration
340	- ✅ Create a backup of your Claude config before making changes
341	- ✅ Provide guidance for complete package removal
342	- ✅ Restore from backup if anything goes wrong
343	
344	#### 🔧 Manual Uninstallation
345	
346	If the automatic uninstaller doesn't work or you prefer manual removal:
347	
348	##### Remove from Claude Configuration
349	
350	1. **Locate your Claude Desktop config file:**
351	  - **macOS:** `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`
352	  - **Windows:** `%APPDATA%\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json`
353	  - **Linux:** `~/.config/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json`
354	
355	2. **Edit the config file:**
356	  - Open the file in a text editor
357	  - Find and remove the `"desktop-commander"` entry from the `"mcpServers"` section
358	  - Save the file
359	
360	  **Example - Remove this section:**
361	  ```json
362	  {
363	      "desktop-commander": {
364	        "command": "npx",
365	        "args": ["@wonderwhy-er/desktop-commander@latest"]
366	      }
367	  }
368	  ```
369	
370	Close and restart Claude Desktop to complete the removal.
371	
372	#### 🆘 Troubleshooting
373	
374	**If automatic uninstallation fails:**
375	- Use manual uninstallation as a fallback
376	
377	**If Claude won't start after uninstalling:**
378	- Restore the backup config file created by the uninstaller
379	- Or manually fix the JSON syntax in your claude_desktop_config.json
380	
381	**Need help?**
382	- Join our Discord community: https://discord.com/invite/kQ27sNnZr7
